From dac125c43baee7d7a3887f45e75763dca33f8225 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: rdavis <ricky.c.davis.9@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 18 Jul 2026 19:03:11 -0500
Subject: [PATCH] Fix speaker/jack audio on Surface Laptop 7 for Business
 (Intel, Lunar Lake)

Two fixes required to get SoundWire audio working on this machine:

1. ASoC: Intel: sof_sdw: append dai type to link names when using
   function topologies.
   No entry in soc-acpi-intel-lnl-match.c can match this firmware:
   it declares two RT1320 instances on link 0 differing only by
   class_id (which snd_soc_acpi_sdw_link_slaves_found() never
   compares, and the duplicate detection forces runtime unique_id to
   SDW_IGNORED_UNIQUE_ID which no 4-bit .adr field can equal), so the
   kernel falls back to the SoundWire default machine driver with
   function topologies. Each link here carries exactly one SDCA
   function (SmartAmp on link 0, UAJ on link 3), so append_dai_type
   stayed false, DAI links got generic "SDW%d-Playback" names,
   sof_sdw_get_tplg_files() could not map them to function topology
   files, and no speaker/jack PCM devices were created. Force
   append_dai_type when the machine uses function topologies (dummy
   monolithic topology filename, the same signal
   snd_sof_load_topology() uses).

2. soundwire: dmi-quirks: drop phantom RT1320.
   Of the two RT1320 _ADR instances (0x000030025D132000 class 00 and
   0x000030025D132001 class 01) only class 01 ever attaches. The
   class 00 phantom still got a sdw_slave device, was aggregated into
   the SmartAmp DAI link, and programming transport params on the
   unattached peripheral failed (-61), aborting prepare on the whole
   Speaker PCM; PipeWire's profile probe then dropped the HiFi
   profile. Remap the phantom _ADR to 0 so the ACPI scan skips it,
   like the existing HP Omen 16 quirk does for its phantom RT1316.

Remaining known gap: internal microphone (firmware exposes SmartMic
SDCA functions on the RT1320s, but codec_info_list only declares an
amp DAI for rt1320).

Co-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 drivers/soundwire/dmi-quirks.c   | 25 +++++++++++++++++++++++++
 sound/soc/intel/boards/sof_sdw.c | 13 +++++++++++++
 2 files changed, 38 insertions(+)

diff --git a/drivers/soundwire/dmi-quirks.c b/drivers/soundwire/dmi-quirks.c
index 5854218e1..dccc5e825 100644
--- a/drivers/soundwire/dmi-quirks.c
+++ b/drivers/soundwire/dmi-quirks.c
@@ -90,6 +90,22 @@ static const struct adr_remap intel_rooks_county[] = {
 	{}
 };
 
+/*
+ * The Microsoft Surface Laptop 7 for Business (Intel, Lunar Lake)
+ * declares two RT1320 instances on link 0 that differ only by
+ * class_id (00 and 01). Only the class_id 01 instance ever attaches
+ * on the bus; the class_id 00 phantom breaks stream setup on the
+ * aggregated amp DAI link (transport params cannot be programmed on
+ * an unattached peripheral). Drop the phantom.
+ */
+static const struct adr_remap surface_laptop_7_intel[] = {
+	{
+		0x000030025d132000ull,
+		0
+	},
+	{}
+};
+
 static const struct dmi_system_id adr_remap_quirk_table[] = {
 	/* TGL devices */
 	{
@@ -164,6 +180,15 @@ static const struct dmi_system_id adr_remap_quirk_table[] = {
 		},
 		.driver_data = (void *)hp_omen_16,
 	},
+	/* LNL devices */
+	{
+		.matches = {
+			DMI_MATCH(DMI_SYS_VENDOR, "Microsoft Corporation"),
+			DMI_MATCH(DMI_PRODUCT_NAME,
+				  "Surface Laptop for Business 7th Edition with Intel"),
+		},
+		.driver_data = (void *)surface_laptop_7_intel,
+	},
 	{}
 };
 
diff --git a/sound/soc/intel/boards/sof_sdw.c b/sound/soc/intel/boards/sof_sdw.c
index 50b838be2..a0f6c1af3 100644
--- a/sound/soc/intel/boards/sof_sdw.c
+++ b/sound/soc/intel/boards/sof_sdw.c
@@ -1246,6 +1246,19 @@ static int sof_card_dai_links_create(struct snd_soc_card *card)
 
 	sdw_be_num = ret;
 
+	/*
+	 * Function topologies are selected by matching the SDCA function
+	 * name embedded in the DAI link name ("SimpleJack", "SmartAmp",
+	 * "SmartMic").  When every link carries a single function,
+	 * append_dai_type stays false and the links get generic
+	 * "SDW%d-Playback" names, so sof_sdw_get_tplg_files() cannot map
+	 * them to function topology files and no PCM devices are created.
+	 * The default machine driver (dummy monolithic topology) has no
+	 * fallback, so always append the dai type there.
+	 */
+	if (mach->sof_tplg_filename && strstr(mach->sof_tplg_filename, "dummy"))
+		ctx->append_dai_type = true;
+
 	/*
 	 * on generic tgl platform, I2S or sdw mode is supported
 	 * based on board rework. A ACPI device is registered in
-- 
